One common way to introduce vulnerabilities is through **Insecure Deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize untrusted data from a user, an attacker can craft a malicious serialized object to execute arbitrary code on the server.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class DeserializationExample {
    // This method is vulnerable because it accepts an unvalidated input stream
    public static Object deserializeData(InputStream is) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(is);
        // Reading an object from an untrusted source can lead to Remote Code Execution (RCE)
        return ois.readObject(); 
    }
}
```

***

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class DeserializationExample {
    // To fix, we use a look-up approach or a library like SerialKiller/Apache Commons IO 
    // to validate classes before they are instantiated.
    public static Object safeDeserialize(InputStream is) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        // Using a ValidatingObjectInputStream (from Apache Commons IO) or checking class types
        // manually prevents the instantiation of malicious gadget chains.
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(is) {
            @Override
            protected Class<?> resolveClass(ObjectStreamClass desc) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
                // Whitelist: Only allow the specific expected class
                if (!desc.getName().equals("com.example.SafeDataClass")) {
                    throw new InvalidClassException("Unauthorized deserialization attempt", desc.getName());
                }
                return super.resolveClass(desc);
            }
        };
        return ois.readObject();
    }
}
```

